WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.680
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.780
Today we are going to have a look&nbsp;
at DeepMind’s Flamingo AI that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:16.500
looked at over 1 billion images, and see&nbsp;
what it had learned from it. And, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:23.820
we will also try to break it. Successfully. Now,&nbsp;
its goal was to fuse AI models that understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:32.460
language with other AI models that understand&nbsp;
images. And what comes out? Absolute magic.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:39.180
For instance, it can be an amazing assistant. We&nbsp;
can give it a picture, ask it what it depicts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:47.760
a bowl of soup with a monster face. I particularly&nbsp;
like this one because this image was also made by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:55.380
a different AI. So this is an AI commenting&nbsp;
on another AI’s work. How cool is that?

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:01.980
Now, we can also subject it to a test that is&nbsp;
interesting because it is designed to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:08.760
the brain of some humans. Look. We need to read&nbsp;
the text and then name the color that was used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:14.940
to write it. If you try to do it quickly, it is&nbsp;
easy to answer it incorrectly, and apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:23.100
this little AI is passing with flying colors.&nbsp;
But it gets better! Look! It knows that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:30.300
is the Stroop test, and it also knows that&nbsp;
humans read this kind of text slower. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:37.620
let’s pop the question: is it challenging for&nbsp;
you too, little AI? “I am not affected by this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:44.700
difference.” Whoa. It is flexing. But,&nbsp;
it backs it up. What a time to be alive!

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:52.020
Now, what it can also do is something called&nbsp;
few-shot learning. What is that? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:59.040
this is learning from only a few examples,&nbsp;
something humans are really good at, but machines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:06.180
not so much. For instance, we look at an image,&nbsp;
and say that this is a chinchilla. We look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:14.760
another image, and say this is a shiba. Then, the&nbsp;
third time, we look at an image, and we say “this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:21.360
is”. So, does it know what it is supposed to do&nbsp;
with this? Remember, neural network-based learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.900
methods typically require thousands and thousands&nbsp;
of training samples to learn something new, so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:35.760
can this new one do it? Yes! Oh boy. That&nbsp;
is amazing. So, this is few-shot learning.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:41.820
And I have a hard time overstating how&nbsp;
incredibly useful it is. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:48.360
we can quickly teach it to read handwritten text&nbsp;
from a piece of paper, or, here comes my favorite:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:56.100
we can even ask it to reverse-engineer the prompts&nbsp;
that the AI used to create these images. We teach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:02.100
it on 2 examples where we tell it the piece&nbsp;
of text that was used to generate it, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:09.540
for the third time, it knows the drill and does it&nbsp;
itself. Or, we can even ask for the ingredients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:17.040
or even the nutrients that are present in a meal,&nbsp;
or what songs a soundtrack of a movie contain.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.060
And, here is another reassuring thing. It does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:26.460
fall for the good old apple + iPod trick&nbsp;
that many previous techniques fell for.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.920
So now, let’s try to answer three&nbsp;
Scholarly questions. Question number one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:38.160
is it really better than previous techniques?
If we compare it to previous zero-shot techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:44.160
this is a challenging case where the AI&nbsp;
has to perform something new, there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:50.400
no contest. The new one is so much better.
However, what I found even more interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:58.260
is that it can even give fine-tuned AIs a run&nbsp;
for their money too. This is insanity. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:05.700
what is that? Fine-tuned means an AI that was&nbsp;
specifically designed and trained for one task,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:13.080
and one task only. This new method can do many&nbsp;
things at the same time, this is a generalist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:19.620
so it is not expected to beat specialized&nbsp;
techniques. How could it? And here comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:25.740
the the insanity part: it is not only competitive&nbsp;
with these fine-tuned techniques on several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:33.480
datasets, but, wow, it even outperforms them&nbsp;
on some of these despite having access to ten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:40.920
and sometimes even a hundred times less training&nbsp;
data. I am stunned. These improvements are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:47.520
concentrated around video-based question&nbsp;
answering datasets like NextQA and iVQA.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.260
Question number two, how does it&nbsp;
improve with the number of shots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:59.220
you remember the flamingo example, we had two&nbsp;
shots here. The answer is that it gets it early,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:04.380
it does not need tens of examples to&nbsp;
understand what we are trying to ask.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:10.680
Its zero-shot performance is formidable, so it&nbsp;
can do things it has never been taught before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:18.180
that is incredible, but even in other cases,&nbsp;
after just a few examples, it really gets it.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:24.300
And, three, the usual suspect.&nbsp;
Model size. Yes! As I hoped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:29.760
results still improve if we train a bigger&nbsp;
neural network, which is great news,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:35.760
two more papers down the line and it will&nbsp;
be so much better, even without algorithmic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:42.060
improvements. If we increase the model size,&nbsp;
it gets smarter, and here is a beautiful chart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:49.260
that showcases that it can learn from only a few&nbsp;
shots significantly better. Dear Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:56.520
this is perhaps artificial intelligence being born&nbsp;
right before our eyes. What a time to be alive!

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:02.640
But wait, we said that it is excellent&nbsp;
at video-based question answering. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:11.100
let’s push it to its limits and see what it can&nbsp;
do. Whoa, that is very nice. It understands that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:19.020
this good boy is being weighed, understands what a&nbsp;
video game avatar means, reads a sequence of text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:24.600
even if only a few letters are shown at&nbsp;
a time as we pan the camera through it.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:30.720
And, hold on to your papers, because it&nbsp;
also understands humor. Yes, that’s right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:36.600
This needs careful prompting, but it can do&nbsp;
it. Here, we are looking at an image where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:42.360
people are getting amused by Obama’s little&nbsp;
prank with the scale. With a little guidance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:47.700
it successfully identifies what is&nbsp;
unusual and amusing about this image.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:53.700
But, we haven’t broken it yet. And now,&nbsp;
you will see that not even this technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:59.820
is perfect. For instance, let’s try to mess&nbsp;
with this little AI and break its brain. How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:06.480
do we do that? Well, of course, with silly,&nbsp;
irrelevant questions. We can even ask it what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:14.520
it can see outside the window? And it says, a&nbsp;
parking lot. Well, not quite! Also, whom is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:21.840
person texting? It says, of course, the driver.&nbsp;
Well, you cannot possibly know that, little AI.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:28.380
And once again, this is an incredible paper,&nbsp;
perhaps a step towards the highly coveted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.380 --> 00:07:35.160
general intelligence that so many people&nbsp;
think we will never reach. I am stunned. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:39.660
what do you think? Does this get your mind&nbsp;
going? Let me know in the comments below!

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:40.500
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

